STATEMENT: >>>
Working state: dev at c8bf73bc (after v0.2.0)

Moved from doc/safety/requirements.yaml by DOC-21 step 3 (content unchanged, decision Q2: this document is the only
source). Relations: Parent with the role DerivedFrom = derived_from (assumed safety goal ASG-* or assumption of use
AoU-*), Parent with the role Specifies = mechanisms (SM-*), a Parent without a role = the BSW requirement the SSR
constrains (none yet). FINDINGS, CODE (one 'file :: symbol' per line) and NOTE as before. Check and matrix: python
tools/trace check, python tools/trace matrix (doc/safety/traceability.md), CI entry safety-trace. The header of
requirements.yaml follows; its traceability chain and the rules on IDs, status, clauses and review status were
brought up to date by DOC-48 (2026-10-04), each change marked there.

Update DOC-43 (step 3b, 2026-10-02): every lettered clause is a CLAUSE node of its own with the UID SSR-nnn(a), its
text, VERIFICATION and TESTS (entries of tools/test/tests.yaml that name the requirement and verify this clause),
and a Parent relation to its SSR; a requirement with clauses has no STATEMENT of its own. A clause is never
renumbered; a dropped clause keeps its UID with STATUS withdrawn. REVIEW_STATUS (proposed, rework, inspected,
accepted) is a field of every SSR, and of a clause where it differs. tools/trace checks the clause nodes (UID,
parent, letters in order, verification within the requirement's, every test entry known and naming the
requirement) and warns about a clause verified by test without a test entry and about a test entry that no clause
claims.

Update DOC-44 (2026-10-02, commit 7c071ae9): the test gaps per clause of DOC-43 were closed (tests allocated to
clauses, two new test entries, SSR-068(c) and SSR-091(e) argued as review) and **11 clauses were added**, review
status proposed: SSR-005(g) ... SSR-005(k) for the command hold of SYS-150/151/161, SSR-020(e), SSR-028(c),
SSR-028(d), SSR-040(e), SSR-051(c) and SSR-088(d). The document now has 59 requirements and 160 clause nodes (159 in
force, SSR-010(d) withdrawn). This change came after the release of 2026-10-02 and is not accepted yet (document
register, anomaly-register.md section 6).

Update DOC-51 (2026-10-04, after the confirmation review CM-07, record doc/safety/reviews/records/CR-07-1.md, the
user under deviation D-01): the inspection **INS-SSR-02** (review section at the end) inspected SSR-086, SSR-089 ...
SSR-092, the 11 clauses of DOC-44 and SSR-024(d) and judged the 27 clauses with more than one "shall" (decision ZD);
their review status is now inspected (SSR-024(d) accepted: R4 confirmed in CM-07). **SM-26 and SM-27 are not credited
for release R1** (mechanisms.sdoc CREDITED no, decision ZD): SSR-089 and SSR-092 specify SM-23 only, SSR-091
specifies SM-34 instead (the withdrawal is a reaction of the safe supervisor to a device fault report), SSR-090 (the
configuration check) specifies no mechanism of the catalogue; their text is unchanged, the NOTE of SSR-089 is brought to the state of the
tree. The findings R3, R4, R5 and R7 of INS-SSR-01 (carried out by DOC-43) were confirmed by the reviewer in
CM-07 (anomalies AN-048 ... AN-050, AN-052 closed). Still 59 requirements and 160 clause nodes.

Update ZE (decision ZE of CM-07, commit 7bec5034 of DOC-51, 2026-10-04): SSR-062 is partial - CODE the age qualifier
that tools/rtegen generates (AgeSupervision, age_link), clause (a) tested by rtegen-age, clause (b) (the event to the
safe supervisor) open with SYS-99 (gap G3), the board proof with SYS-28. tools/trace check warns "SSR-062(b)
verification test, no test entry"; the reviewer accepted this warning until SYS-99 (re-check of CR-07-1, ZE).

Re-check of CR-07-1 (2026-10-04, the user under deviation D-01, at dev 898dbcd1): INS-SSR-02 confirmed (R4, ZD; it is
an author-side inspection, the re-check is its check), the allocation of SSR-091 to SM-34 accepted; this document is
released with concept issue 3 (verdict accepted, CM-07 closed). Header and state line brought up by DOC-57.

.. code-block:: text

    neXtBSW software safety requirements (SYS-22) - stage 1, ASIL B, Safety Element out of Context.

    Decision of the user 2026-09-21 (decision board `safety-anforderungen`): requirements as YAML in the repository
    with stable IDs, linked to code and to tests, matrix generated and checked in the CI.

    Traceability chain (DOC-48: the catalogue nodes of DOC-21 step 3 instead of the concept tables):
      ASG-* / AoU-*  (doc/requirements/safety/goals.sdoc and assumptions.sdoc, moved there from concept.md
                      sections 2.2 and 4; the proposed AoU-24/25 of fmea.md section 6 are nodes there too)
        -> SSR-*     (this file; Parent relation with the role DerivedFrom)
        -> SSR-nnn(x) (CLAUSE nodes of this file, Parent relation to their SSR; since DOC-43)
        -> SM-*      (doc/requirements/safety/mechanisms.sdoc; Parent relation with the role Specifies) and
                      findings / gaps / tasks F*, G*, T*, N* (FINDINGS)
        -> code      (CODE: file :: symbol, checked for existence; plus `// nx-req: SSR-nnn` tags in the code)
        -> tests     (tools/test/tests.yaml: field `requirements: [SSR-nnn, ...]` of a test entry; per clause the
                      field TESTS)
    Check and matrix: `python tools/trace check`, `python tools/trace matrix` (doc/safety/traceability.md); CI entry
    `safety-trace` in tools/test/tests.yaml.

    Rules
    - IDs are stable: an ID is never reused or renumbered. A requirement or clause that is dropped keeps its ID
      with STATUS withdrawn (status value since DOC-43; first use SSR-010(d)).
    - Stage 1 credits base-software mechanisms only (user, `abn-sys19`, concept section 1.4): a stage-1 requirement
      must not name SM-33, SM-52 or SM-40 ... SM-43; the check refuses it.
    - status: implemented (in the code, no known limit), partial (in the code, with a known limit named in `findings`),
      open (not in the code yet - a proposed task or gap; no test expected), withdrawn (dropped, ID kept; DOC-43).
    - verification: test | review | analysis | board. "test" means an entry of tools/test/tests.yaml must name the
      requirement; review-only evidence is reported as "requirement without test" (fmea.md F15).
    - No invented numbers (concept section 0): every time below is a code constant, a value derived from one, or a
      configured value, and says so.
    - Text in English, like the rest of doc/ (CLAUDE.md, user 2026-09-17).
    - `text` is free of implementation detail (SYS-72): it names the element that carries the requirement (safe
      supervisor, safety task, generator, build, bootloader ...) and the observable behaviour, but no function, class,
      constant, event identifier, file, register or configuration key. Those go to `note` ("Realisation: ...") and to
      `code`. Other requirements are cited by their ID.
    - `text` is atomic (SYS-72): one statement with one "shall" per clause. A requirement that still bundles several
      statements lists them as lettered clauses (a), (b) ..., each verifiable on its own and citable as SSR-nnn(a);
      since DOC-43 every clause is a CLAUSE node of its own whose UID is that citation, SSR-nnn(x) (no new IDs).
      A check together with the event it raises on a failure counts as one statement.
    - Review status: the field REVIEW_STATUS of every SSR (proposed, rework, inspected, accepted), and of a clause
      where it differs (since DOC-43). The inspection record stays in the section "Review status and inspection
      record" at the end of this file.
<<<

