<<<
NOTE: >>>
SYS-140, decision C4 (doc/safety/partner-and-degraded.md 5.9). Realisation: NX_SAFE_BRIDGE_FAULT_CLASSES(X)
X(OUTPUT) X(SUPPLY) X(INTERNAL) in safeSupervisor/Degraded.h; SafeSupervisor::handle() calls
blockShortOnBridgeFault() first in the case DEVICE_FAULT, under the interrupt lock, before the log line, SD, S1
held and S1 + S2; the devices bound to a group with a short (board pwm[].gate_driver) are derived by
tools/nxconfig/safestate.py (NX_MODULE_SAFESUPERVISOR_DERIVED_BRIDGE_FAULT_DEVICE_<n>: instance << 8 | selection
groups); the withdrawal itself is the family's nxSafeStateBlockShort() (safeSupervisor/SafeStateShort.h). The
other two sources of 5.9: the hardware fault input forces freewheel by itself and the family port withdraws the
short when it sees the fault latch or a finding of the threshold path (SSR-092); a failed start test withdraws
it in the selection service (SSR-089 (g)). Effective only for devices whose package raises DEVICE_FAULT (SYS-28);
the TMC6200 driver switches its enable off by itself on its shutdown faults (freewheel at the bridge). Host
test safe-supervisor-bridge-fault with 14 mutants; board proof open (fmea.md B13).
Clause (e): the generator half is tested by nxconfig-safestate (a project without an active short gets no derived
bridge device word, the demo is unchanged); that the supervisor of such a project is built as before is verified by
review of the compile-time guard in the safe supervisor's build (DOC-44, no test compares the two images).
*Issue 3 (DOC-51):* SM-26 is not credited for release R1 (CM-07 decision ZD); the Specifies relation to SM-26 was
replaced by SM-34, because the withdrawal is a reaction of the safe supervisor to a device fault report.
<<<
