NOTE: >>>
SYS-139, decision C2 (a) (doc/safety/partner-and-degraded.md 5.4, 5.7). Realisation: NX_SAFE_STATE_SHORT_HOLD(X)
X(gdTmc6200) in integration/SafeStateHold.h; ShortHold.h (group in a short = the walk's value for the timer
entry differs from freewheel); the lock of a bound TMC6200 is shortHold::lock(<base lock>, enabled), its adapter
keeps DRV_EN in switchOffAfterDebugHalt() and reports it held; FtmPwmInhibit writes forcedValue(); the group lock
of SYS-123 in ComplementaryPwm writes ftmPwm::lockedOutputControl(); HwThresholdWatch withdraws the short on a
finding or on FMS[FAULTF]. Only compiled in a project with an active short (NX_SAFE_OUTPUT_SELECT); every other
project is built as before. The generic adapter of the generated binding (ARCH-52) keeps no enable: the
configuration check refuses a short there. Host test short-hold with 9 mutants; board proof open (fmea.md B12-B14).
Clause (d): short-hold shows the withdrawal itself, short-withdraw-order (DOC-44, source test with its own mutants)
shows that the threshold watch calls it for a finding or the timer's fault latch and before it inhibits the group;
clause (e) is tested by nxconfig-safestate. *Issue 3 (DOC-51):* SM-26 is not credited for release R1 (CM-07
decision ZD); this requirement specifies SM-23, whose lock and inhibit paths it extends (the Specifies relation to
SM-26 was removed).
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ASG-01
  ROLE: DerivedFrom
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ASG-02
  ROLE: DerivedFrom
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: AoU-04
  ROLE: DerivedFrom
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SM-23
  ROLE: Specifies

[CLAUSE]
UID: SSR-092(a)
STATEMENT: >>>
While a PWM group is in an active short (SSR-089: the walks write its short), the gate-driver enable bound
to the group shall not be switched off by the safe state lock of its driver, by the lock of a degraded group or
by the debug halt guard, as long as that enable was on; an enable that was off shall stay off, and every enable
request in S1 shall be refused.
<<<
VERIFICATION: test, board
TESTS: short-hold, short-hold-mutations
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SSR-092

[CLAUSE]
UID: SSR-092(b)
STATEMENT: >>>
Every path of the family port that forces the group's timer outside the table walk - the PWM inhibit of the
debug halt guard and of the hold of S1, and the lock of a degraded group - shall write the output control the
walks write for the group, never freewheel over a selected short.
<<<
VERIFICATION: test, board
TESTS: short-hold, short-hold-mutations
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SSR-092

[CLAUSE]
UID: SSR-092(c)
STATEMENT: >>>
When the short is withdrawn, the clock of the group's timer is off or the selection is not exactly an allowed
pattern, (a) and (b) shall give the behaviour without an active short: freewheel and the enable off within one
poll of the hold.
<<<
VERIFICATION: test, board
TESTS: short-hold
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SSR-092

[CLAUSE]
UID: SSR-092(d)
STATEMENT: >>>
A finding of the group's hardware threshold path or its latched fault input shall withdraw the short of the
group (SSR-089 (f)) before the family port forces the group.
<<<
VERIFICATION: test, board
TESTS: short-hold, short-withdraw-order
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SSR-092

[CLAUSE]
UID: SSR-092(e)
STATEMENT: >>>
The modules whose bound enable the family port keeps shall be a fact of the family port that the
configuration check reads; the configuration check shall refuse an active short whose bound enable belongs to
another module, and every active short while the fact is missing.
<<<
VERIFICATION: test, board
TESTS: nxconfig-safestate
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SSR-092

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Review status and inspection record

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Moved verbatim from the section ``review`` of doc/safety/requirements.yaml (SYS-72). tools/trace does not read it.
Since DOC-43 (step 3b) the review status of every requirement is the field REVIEW_STATUS of its node (of a clause
only where it differs from its requirement); the map ``requirements`` below keeps the changes and remarks.

.. code-block:: yaml

    # ==================================================================================================================
    # Review status and inspection record (SYS-72, from doc/safety/iso26262-gap.md section 6 no. 17, rows 8-06, 8-07,
    # 8-10). tools/trace ignores this section; it does not change the matrix. Proposal for tools/trace (outside SYS-72):
    # accept a per-requirement key `review` with the values below, check that every requirement has one, and show it as a
    # matrix column; DOC-21 step 3 carries it as a StrictDoc field REVIEW_STATUS in doc/requirements/nextbsw.sgra.
    # ==================================================================================================================

    review:
      statuses:
        proposed: written, not inspected yet
        rework: >-
          inspected; at least one finding is open (listed under `open` below) and needs a change outside this file
          or a decision
        inspected: >-
          inspected against the checklist, every finding closed in this file; waits for the independent
          confirmation review (iso26262-gap.md section 6 no. 4, question Q1)
        accepted: >-
          confirmed by an independent reviewer (independence I1 at least); the record names the reviewer. Since
          2026-09-30 (AN-093, safety plan tailoring T-11) also: accepted by the user as safety responsible, without
          independence - the record says so in `independence`, and such an acceptance gives no ASIL statement until an
          independent review confirms it

      checklist:
        # Characteristics of a single requirement and properties of the set, in own words after ISO 26262 part 8 clauses
        # 6.4.2.4 and 6.4.3; the method is an inspection (part 8 table 2).
        - {id: C1, name: implementation-free, question: "Does the text name only the element and the observable behaviour - no function, class, constant, event identifier, file, register or configuration key?"}
        - {id: C2, name: atomic, question: "Does every clause make exactly one statement with one 'shall' (a check with its event counts as one)?"}
        - {id: C3, name: unambiguous, question: "Is every term defined here, in concept.md or by a cited SSR (no 'safety-relevant registers' without a list)?"}
        - {id: C4, name: verifiable, question: "Can every clause be shown by test, analysis, review or a board check, and is it a property of the product rather than of the process?"}
        - {id: C5, name: consistent, question: "Does the text agree with the note, the linked code and the other SSR (no contradiction, no silent duplication)?"}
        - {id: C6, name: complete, question: "Are the conditions under which the behaviour ends, repeats or is cleared stated?"}
        - {id: C7, name: portable, question: "Does the text hold on a board without SBC, with another derivative and with several CAN buses (review questions of doc/architecture section 11)?"}
        - {id: C8, name: traceable, question: "Are derived_from, mechanisms and code unchanged or deliberately changed, and does the title still fit?"}

      records:
        - id: INS-SSR-01
          date: 2026-09-28
          baseline: origin/dev 20b2e04 (before), branch claude/sys-72 (after)
          scope: all 52 requirements of this file, text, title and note; checklist C1-C8
          method: >-
            inspection by reading every requirement against the checklist, plus two automatic checks - a search of
            every text for code identifiers (names with :: or (), all-caps names with an underscore, camelCase words,
            configuration paths, file endings, back-quoted keys; 0 hits after the rework) and a comparison of every field
            except text and note with the baseline (0 differences), so that the matrix and the test links stay unchanged
          inspector: cloud session SYS-72 (an agent, like the authors of the SSR)
          independence: >-
            none - this record is an author-side inspection and does not replace the confirmation review at
            I1 (iso26262-gap.md section 6 no. 4, open question Q1)
          result:
            requirements: 52
            texts_changed: 50 (SSR-080 and SSR-083 passed unchanged)
            implementation_detail_removed: >-
              25 texts named a function, constant, event identifier, file or configuration
              key; moved to `note` as "Realisation" (the code links were already there)
            split_into_clauses: >-
              36 texts now list lettered clauses (a) ... (f), 116 clauses in total; 16 texts are one
              statement
            made_precise: >-
              SSR-033, SSR-034 name the lock and check list of SSR-070 instead of "safety-relevant registers";
              SSR-031, SSR-069 name the channel and the event of SSR-030 / SSR-032; SSR-002, SSR-010, SSR-016, SSR-028
              say "complete S1" instead of "walk the table"; SSR-084 says what a disagreement causes
            completed: >-
              SSR-065(c) states when the count is cleared (behaviour documented in the note since SYS-43 and
              covered by its tests); SSR-066(b) states the reaction S1 + S2 of the deadman check (from the note)
            status_count: {inspected: 46, rework: 6, accepted: 0}
          open:
            - {id: R1, ssr: SSR-068, check: C4, finding: "clause (c) 'shall be decided and documented' is a process statement, not a property of the product", proposal: "state the decision of SYS-41 as a requirement - the start-up writes every RAM word before its first read, so that the ECC is valid (no RAM test at start) - with verification review of the start-up code"}
            - {id: R2, ssr: SSR-050, check: C7, finding: "clause (a) assumes a power-supply role; on a board without one (no SBC) the text does not say what the start reason is taken from", proposal: "add: without a power-supply role the MCU's reset status is the only source and the rank rule reduces to it; check with the ecuState host tests (profile without SBC)"}
            - {id: R3, ssr: "SSR-010, SSR-028", check: C5, finding: "SSR-010(d) (S1 before the watchdog reset) repeats SSR-028(a) for the watchdog warning", proposal: "at DOC-21 step 3 drop SSR-010(d) and move its test link (safe-outputs-order) to SSR-028 in tools/test/tests.yaml"}
            - {id: R4, ssr: "SSR-024, SSR-067", check: C5, finding: "SSR-024(d) (re-check period) repeats SSR-067(a)", proposal: "at DOC-21 step 3 keep the period only in SSR-067 and let SSR-024(d) say once per start"}
            - {id: R5, ssr: SSR-007, check: C1, finding: "the title names the configuration keyword safe_off", proposal: "title 'One power-stage adapter per switched-off output, enforced by the build' (changes the matrix: regenerate traceability.md in the same commit)"}
            - {id: R6, ssr: SSR-016, check: C7, finding: "clause (b) states the family value 10 ms in the text", proposal: "keep the value out of the text ('the deadman time-out of the family port') and list it in the HSI (hsi-s32k1xx.md) and the safety manual, where the integrator's FTTI check reads it"}
            - {id: R7, ssr: all, check: C4, finding: "verification and test links are given per requirement, not per clause; whether every clause has its own evidence is not shown", proposal: "at DOC-21 step 3 give every clause node its own VERIFICATION and test links; a clause without evidence becomes a warning of tools/trace"}
        - id: REV-SYS117-R9
          date: 2026-09-30
          baseline: integ/night4 with SYS-117 (bf095f44), texts unchanged since
          scope: SSR-087, SSR-088 and clause (b) of SSR-001 (degraded reaction SD, SYS-117); text, derived_from, mechanisms
          method: >-
            safety review of SYS-117 by the user as safety responsible (decision board review-sys117, item R9 "Freigeben
            (Status accepted)"); R1 ... R12 answered as recommended, R8 released the concept texts AoU-04, AoU-32, the
            paragraph of concept 2.2 and the SD paragraph of concept section 3
          inspector: the user (safety responsible)
          independence: >-
            none - the user is author side (commissioning approver); recorded under the deviation of the safety plan
            T-11 (AN-093). The acceptance does not replace a confirmation review at I1
          result:
            requirements: 3 (SSR-087, SSR-088, SSR-001 clause (b))
            status_count: {accepted: 2, accepted_clause: 1}
          open: []

        - id: DOC-43
          date: 2026-10-02
          baseline: integ/night11 baa1d837 (before), branch agent/DOC-43 (after)
          scope: >-
            all 59 requirements - lettered clauses as nodes of their own with verification and test entries per
            clause (R7), the findings R3, R4 and R5 of INS-SSR-01, the review status as the field REVIEW_STATUS
          method: >-
            conversion by tools/trace/migrate_clauses.py with an equivalence check (--verify: every text equal word
            for word after the clauses are joined again, every other field equal, apart from the changes listed
            under result); the allocation of the test entries to the clauses read from the test comments of
            tools/test/tests.yaml and, where unclear, from the test sources
          inspector: agent session DOC-43 (author side, like the authors of the SSR)
          independence: >-
            none - the closures of R3, R4, R5 and R7 and the allocation of tests to clauses wait for the acceptance
            of the safety responsible (safety review of DOC-43)
          result:
            requirements: 59
            clause_nodes: 149 (148 in force, SSR-010(d) withdrawn)
            closes: [R3, R4, R5, R7]
            changes: >-
              R3 SSR-010(d) withdrawn (repeated SSR-028(a)); R4 SSR-024(d) states the pass once per start and leaves
              the repetition to SSR-067 (new wording, REVIEW_STATUS proposed); R5 title of SSR-007 without the
              configuration keyword; R7 VERIFICATION and TESTS per clause, tools/trace warns about a clause whose
              verification names test without a test entry and about a test entry of a requirement that no clause
              claims
          open: []

        - id: INS-SSR-02
          date: 2026-10-04
          baseline: dev aaa22c86 (= dev 5cc50658 for doc/requirements, the baseline of CM-07), branch agent/DOC-51 (after)
          scope: >-
            the requirements and clauses that were still proposed at the baseline - SSR-086, SSR-089, SSR-090,
            SSR-091, SSR-092 (text, title, note, relations), the 11 clauses of DOC-44 (SSR-005(g) ... SSR-005(k),
            SSR-020(e), SSR-028(c), SSR-028(d), SSR-040(e), SSR-051(c), SSR-088(d)) and SSR-024(d) of DOC-43; and the
            27 clauses that test_requirements_quality.py reports with more than one "shall"; checklist C1-C8
          method: >-
            inspection by reading every text against the checklist and the code links (CODE fields exist, tools/trace
            check 0 errors), plus the two automatic checks of DOC-48 (test_requirements_quality.py: no code
            identifier in a text, a valid review status on every node); ordered by the decision ZD of the
            confirmation review CM-07 (record doc/safety/reviews/records/CR-07-1.md, 2026-10-04)
          inspector: agent session DOC-51 (author side, like the authors of the SSR)
          independence: >-
            none - an author-side inspection; it is re-checked by the user (D-01, without independence) in the
            re-check of CR-07-1 and does not replace a confirmation review at I1; confirmed in that re-check on
            2026-10-04 (R4, ZD)
          result:
            inspected: >-
              SSR-086, SSR-089 ... SSR-092 and the 11 DOC-44 clauses meet C1 (no code identifier; the realisation is
              in the notes), C3, C4, C6, C7 and C8; review status inspected. SSR-024(d) (R4 wording of DOC-43):
              accepted - the reviewer confirmed R4 in CM-07 (decision ZD)
            consistency_C5: >-
              the NOTE of SSR-089 was stale (configuration check refusing every short, bridge-fault rule, hold of the
              short, RTE port and start-test call as follow-ups) - brought to the state of the tree (SYS-139, SYS-140,
              SYS-141, SYS-143); SM-26 and SM-27 not credited for R1: SSR-089 and SSR-092 specify SM-23, SSR-091
              SM-34, SSR-090 no mechanism (decision ZD); texts unchanged
            atomicity_C2: >-
              of the 27 clauses with more than one "shall", 20 are one statement each: a check together with its
              event (SSR-012, SSR-018(e), SSR-021(a), SSR-021(b), SSR-023, SSR-032, SSR-033(b), SSR-066(b),
              SSR-080, SSR-084, SSR-086(a)), or one property stated with its limit or its negative (SSR-001(a),
              SSR-028(c), SSR-040(c), SSR-067(a), SSR-087(b), SSR-087(c), SSR-087(g), SSR-089(d), SSR-089(f)).
              Seven bundle independent statements, each verified by the tests of its clause: SSR-064(b) (reaction
              S3; reachable from any context), SSR-087(f) (re-apply; implausible record; power-on clears),
              SSR-089(c) (bound enable kept; other outputs inactive; no output on), SSR-089(e) (acceptance rule;
              refused request; freewheel after reset), SSR-091(d) and SSR-092(e) (fact of the code; refusal by the
              configuration check), SSR-092(a) (enable kept; enable off stays off; request refused in S1). They are
              remarks, not findings: split them at the next content change of the requirement, after release R1
              (clause IDs are stable; the clause count of 160 is pinned by tools/trace/test_trace_sdoc.py)
            status_count: {inspected_requirements: 5, inspected_clauses: 11, accepted_clauses: 1, findings: 0}
          confirms: [R3, R4, R5, R7]
          open: []
